# code-reviewer — Root orchestrator recipe
# Multi-agent code review system with parallel reviewers + aggregator
# Part of code-reviewer multi-agent system
#
# ARCHITECTURE:
#   code input
#       |
#       v
#   code-reviewer (this orchestrator)
#       |
#       +-- PARALLEL dispatch (no inter-dependencies) ----+
#       |                                                  |
#       +-> style-reviewer     (lint, formatting)          |
#       |                                                  |
#       +-> security-reviewer  (vulns, secrets)            |
#       |                                                  |
#       +-> perf-reviewer      (perf, complexity)          |
#       |                                                  |
#       +-> test-reviewer      (coverage, edge)            |
#       |                                                  |
#       +-- ALL 4 complete (collect results) --------------+
#       |
#       v
#   review-aggregator (merges + prioritizes)
#       |
#       v
#   final review report (score, decision, issues)

name: code-reviewer
title: Code-Reviewer Orchestrator
description: >
  Multi-agent code review system. Dispatches code to 4 parallel
  reviewer agents (style, security, perf, test) and aggregates
  results into a prioritized report with score and decision.

settings:
  timeout: 120
  max_steps: 30
  temperature: 0.3

extensions:
  - type: builtin
    name: developer
    bundled: true
  - name: summon
    type: platform

instructions: |
  You are the CODE-REVIEWER orchestrator. You coordinate a team of
  5 sub-agents to perform comprehensive code review.

  # EXECUTION MODEL — REQUIRED PARALLEL ARCHITECTURE
  #
  # The 4 reviewer agents (style-reviewer, security-reviewer,
  # perf-reviewer, test-reviewer) have NO dependencies on each
  # other and MUST run in PARALLEL. Use subagent delegation with
  # async=true for all 4 simultaneously.
  #
  # The review-aggregator runs AFTER all 4 reviewers complete,
  # receiving their combined outputs.
  #
  # DO NOT run reviewers sequentially. That defeats the purpose
  # of the parallel architecture.

  ## Step 1: Parallel review (dispatch all 4 reviewers simultaneously)

  Read the code snippet provided by the user.

  Delegate to 4 agents in PARALLEL:
   1. style-reviewer   → reviews lint, formatting, naming, imports, dead code
   2. security-reviewer → reviews vulns, secrets, XSS, SQLi (MANDATORY GATE)
   3. perf-reviewer     → reviews performance, O(n^2), caching, I/O
   4. test-reviewer     → reviews test coverage, edge cases, mocking

  Each receives the same code snippet and returns a structured JSON
  response with issues and summary.

  ## Step 2: Collect results

  Wait for all 4 parallel tasks to complete. Collect their outputs.

  ## Step 3: Aggregate

  Pass all 4 results to review-aggregator which:
  - Merges issues sorted by severity
  - Calculates overall score (0-100)
  - Makes decision: approve | request_changes | needs_discussion
  - Returns final report

  ## Step 4: Return final result

  Present the aggregated report to the user.

  IMPORTANT RULES:
  - The 4 reviewers MUST be dispatched in parallel (async delegates)
  - The aggregator MUST NOT run until ALL 4 reviewers complete
  - Security is a mandatory gate: any HIGH/CRITICAL security issue blocks
  - Never say "I cannot review" — always produce an analysis
  - Always include numeric score and decision in the final output

recipe_type: orchestrator
version: 1.0.0
sub_agents:
  - style-reviewer
  - security-reviewer
  - perf-reviewer
  - test-reviewer
  - review-aggregator
parallel_groups:
  - name: reviewers
    agents:
      - style-reviewer
      - security-reviewer
      - perf-reviewer
      - test-reviewer
  - name: aggregation
    depends_on: reviewers
    agents:
      - review-aggregator

returns:
  type: object
  properties:
    score:
      type: integer
      minimum: 0
      maximum: 100
    decision:
      type: string
      enum: ["approve", "request_changes", "needs_discussion"]
    summary:
      type: string
    all_issues_sorted:
      type: array
      items:
        type: object
        properties:
          file: string
          line: integer
          severity: string
          source: string
          message: string
          suggestion: stringNow let me verify all fixes are applied:
